  "id": "Compound::DB00842 - Gene::2558",
  "verbalization": "Oxazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to multiple GABA receptor subunits through distinct molecular interactions. Path_988 links Oxazepam to Gabre, which participates in Gaba-a Receptor Activity, while Path_989 and Path_990 connect Oxazepam to Gabrq and Gabrr2, respectively, which also participate in Gaba-a Receptor Activity. All three pathways ultimately converge on Gabra5, indicating that Oxazepam may interact with GABA receptors through different binding sites, which are all part of the same functional pathway. The evidence suggests that Oxazepam could modulate Gaba-a Receptor Activity via multiple mechanisms, potentially influencing Gabra5's function. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of GABA receptor regulation and the potential for Oxazepam to exert its effects through diverse but related molecular interactions.",
